  "id": "upright-row",
  "name": "Upright Row",
  "definition": "A strict bilateral compound pull from a low cable in which the bar travels vertically while both elbows flex and the upper arms elevate through a catalog-authored mixture of shoulder flexion and abduction. The initial record preserves the Lorenzetti straight-bar topology and elbow-at-shoulder-height endpoint without publishing numeric shoulder angles: the study reports substantial sagittal and frontal shoulder excursion, but its Rab-style Euler decomposition becomes indeterminate near 90 degrees of abduction and therefore cannot establish unique sagittal or axial magnitudes. Scapular upward rotation and posterior tilt are bounded kinematic transfers rather than direct upright-row measurements. Ludewig directly observed both coupled actions during flexion and abduction, while loaded arm-elevation evidence supports retaining upward rotation under external resistance; neither source establishes a fixed scapulohumeral ratio for this cable fixture. McAllister supports upright-row recruitment of the anterior, lateral, and posterior deltoids, upper and middle trapezius, and biceps only. Its barbell grip-width experiment does not rank muscles against one another, determine joint actions, or transfer grip effects to this cable record. Accordingly, lateral deltoid remains the primary shoulder emphasis, posterior deltoid and middle trapezius remain stabilizers, and no shoulder axial-rotation, scapular-retraction, or scapular-elevation prime action is inferred. The source-observed spinal behavior is nonstandardized rather than position-held. Shrugs, angle-held raises, horizontal or extension rows, presses, high pulls, and any deliberately leg-driven version remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "lorenzetti-2017-pulling-exercise-kinematics",
    "mcallister-2013-upright-row-grip-width",
    "eldridge-2024-loaded-scapular-elevation",
    "ludewig-2009-multiplanar-humeral-elevation",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work"
  ],
